@meta
id: 29-14
title: Drill — open it without the path
chapter: 29
type: drill
skills: fuzzy-finder
par: 23
minutes: 5
reps: 8
vscode: Ctrl-P, type three letters, Enter — the same muscle, a different key
open: notes.md
vimrc: set packpath^=. | packloadall!

@brief
Eight times: open `src/infrastructure/services/refund.service.ts` without
typing its path, delete the leftover `DEBUG` flag on its first line, save,
quit. The finder plugin ships with the lesson, in `pack/dojo/start/files/`.

The point of the drill is the fragment, not the edit. Pick three or four
letters that pin the file down and stop thinking about directories.

@keys
| `:Files {fragment}` | open the best fuzzy match |
| `dd` | delete the line |

@hints
`refsvc` is four letters of `refund.service` and matches nothing else in
this tree.
--
`:Files refsvc` `Enter`, `dd`, `:wq` `Enter`.

@solution
    :Files refsvc<Enter>  dd  :wq<Enter>

Twenty keystrokes.

By rep four you should have stopped composing the fragment carefully and
started throwing letters at it. That is the right instinct: a fuzzy
finder is cheap to be wrong with, because a bad guess costs you one Esc.
The expensive habit is the one it replaces — opening a file tree, finding
the folder, scrolling, clicking.

The fragment that works is usually the initials of the words in the name,
with the directory implied: `refsvc`, `ordprov`, `custrepo`. If two files
collide, add a letter from the directory.

@start pack/dojo/start/files/plugin/files.vim
" dojo-files.vim - a fuzzy file finder in thirty lines.
" Shipped inside this lesson. You did not install it; the dojo did.
"
"   :Files {pattern}   open the file whose path best matches {pattern}
"   :Files <Tab>       cycle the candidates on the command line
"
" The ranking is matchfuzzy(), which vim has had built in since 8.2. The
" letters you type have to appear in the path in order, and nothing else.
" `opr` finds order.provider.ts. That is the whole trick fzf sells.

if exists('g:loaded_dojo_files')
  finish
endif
let g:loaded_dojo_files = 1

function! s:index() abort
  let all = split(globpath('.', '**/*'), "\n")
  call filter(all, 'filereadable(v:val)')
  call map(all, 'substitute(v:val, "^\\./", "", "")')
  " A real finder skips .git, node_modules and build output. This one skips
  " the package directory, so the list is the project and nothing else.
  return filter(all, 'v:val !~# "^pack/"')
endfunction

function! s:matches(pat) abort
  let all = s:index()
  return a:pat ==# '' ? all : matchfuzzy(all, a:pat)
endfunction

function! s:complete(lead, line, pos) abort
  return s:matches(a:lead)
endfunction

function! s:files(pat) abort
  let hits = s:matches(a:pat)
  if empty(hits)
    echohl ErrorMsg
    echo 'Files: nothing matches ' . a:pat
    echohl NONE
    return
  endif
  execute 'edit ' . fnameescape(hits[0])
endfunction

command! -nargs=? -complete=customlist,s:complete Files call s:files(<q-args>)

